      { id: "m1-a1", title: "What is Web application?", tags: ["Basics", "HTTP", "Architecture"], intro: "Understand what a web application is and how it works in ASP.NET MVC projects.", blocks: [
        { heading: "Overview", html: `<p>A <b>web application</b> is software that runs on a <b>web server</b> and is accessed through a browser.</p><p>Unlike static websites, users can interact with a web application by logging in, searching, submitting forms, and viewing personalized data.</p>` },
        { heading: "How a Web Application Works", html: `<ol><li><b>Browser Request:</b> User sends an HTTP request (URL, form submit, API call).</li><li><b>Server Processing:</b> Server-side code validates input, runs business logic, and talks to a database.</li><li><b>Response:</b> Server returns HTML/JSON that the browser renders for the user.</li></ol>` },
        { heading: "ASP.NET MVC Perspective", html: `<p>In ASP.NET MVC, the flow is typically:</p><ul><li><b>Controller</b> handles incoming request.</li><li><b>Model</b> contains business/data logic.</li><li><b>View</b> renders the final UI.</li></ul><p>This separation keeps code maintainable and aligns with enterprise project structure.</p>` },
        { heading: "Website vs Web Application", html: `<table class="table table-sm"><thead><tr><th>Website</th><th>Web Application</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Mainly informational pages</td><td>Interactive features and workflows</td></tr><tr><td>Minimal user input</td><td>Heavy user input and processing</td></tr><tr><td>Content-focused</td><td>Task-focused (login, booking, dashboard, etc.)</td></tr></tbody></table>` }
